{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791578802,"nonce":"GCWumjoBb4pDOajPrq0pyGBFGSU=","title":"A real symbol can take SymbolId(0), the id MIR uses for this, and lend this its type","message":"MIR binds a method's `this` under SymbolId(0) (lower_param_default, dispatch, variables.rs). The symbol table allocated ids from 0, so whichever symbol a compilation created first got that id too. On the BLADE-cached path that is `Any` (abstract over Dynamic). An assignment `this = new AtomicIntData(v)` in AtomicInt's constructor then read its target type from symbol 0 = Any, stored into Dynamic storage, and boxed the object (haxe_box_reference_ptr). AtomicInt.load read slot 2 of the box: haxe.EventLoop.numPendingThreadTasks.load() returned garbage (1702388072), EventLoop.loop() waited forever, and every program that pulls in haxe.EntryPoint hung after main (the 0ffcf22 exit hang). --no-cache created a different first symbol, so it did not reproduce there. Bisect of the AtomicInt probe pointed at e9690830 only because that commit changed which symbol was created first.\n\nProbe: new haxe.atomic.AtomicInt(7).load() through --cache-dir prints nothing and hangs; uncached prints load=7.\n\nFix: SymbolId(0) is never allocated (SymbolTable::next_symbol_id starts at 1).","files":null}]}